  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1029 PM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nBatch of showers working west to east but should fizzle away by \nsunrise. Some lingering showery debris possible Saturday morning. \nAnomalous moisture will still be overhead with a focus on \nlocations along the Int'l Border. Lower PW values over the \nnorthern half of the CONUS (0.7\"-0.8\") and higher values down \nsouth (0.9\"-1.1\"). Expect mostly cloudy skies Saturday. No real \nforcing for storms on Saturday, even though we have the moisture. \nIf there's any breaks in the clouds, maybe a shower or \nthunderstorm possible. HRRR is suggesting some type of activity \ngenerally Deming and west. Halloween does look like it'll feature \ngood weather for many locations. Highs will reach to the low to \nmid 70s but will dip into the 60s after dark. Winds stay light \nthrough the day and evening.\n\nThe next round of showers and thunderstorms exists Sunday evening. A \nsouthern Pacific low will meet up with a more northern Pacific low, \njoining up over the desert SW. Moisture is still well above normal \nwith PW values of 0.9\"-1.1\". Now this time, we will have synoptic \nlevel forcing for storms Sunday evening. Uncertain how much \ninstability there will be. GFS suggests very low CAPE values, but \nthe EC suggest CAPEs of 500-1000 J/kg. After further digging, the \nNBM and LREF has more modest CAPE values (LREF ~500 J/kg, NBM ~600-\n1000 J/kg) with a focus over Hudspeth  and S. Otero counties. \nHowever, this area of instability may not be influenced just yet but \nthe synoptic forcing. One thing we will have plenty of is shear. \nWill also have a pacific cold front moving through with this trough \nlate Sunday and into Monday. May just have more showery activity \nwith embedded thunderstorms that evening. Rain chances will push \neastward through the overnight with some lingering showery activity \npossible Monday morning and afternoon. In addition to rain chances, \nwinds will be breezy Sunday afternoon at 15-25 mph with gusts to 40 \nmph. \n\nBy Monday afternoon, the center of the low will cross northern NM, \nsweeping out any moisture leftover. Winds will continue to be breezy \nMonday afternoon with similar wind speeds to Sunday. A much needed \nstretch of dry weather is ahead Tuesday through at least Friday.",
